  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Valley rain and mountain snow Sunday into Monday morning,\n   focused along and south of I-90. Northwest Montana stays mostly\n   dry.  \n\n-  High pressure bringing stable conditions and valley fog next\n   week. \n\n-  Potential return to unsettled pattern with chances for mountain\n   snow by next weekend (Nov. 7-8).\n\nA broad low pressure system continues to spin along the Pacific\nNorthwest coast this morning, with southerly flow transporting\natmospheric moisture and cloud cover across the Northern Rockies.\nShower activity has been observed across Lemhi County, ID into \nsouthwest Montana (along the divide) this morning and that trend \nwill continue through the evening. Most locations along and north\nof the I-90 will stay dry this evening.\n\nHeading into Sunday, the low pressure system will push south and\neast, moving towards the Four Corners region. Forecast guidance\nhas trended this low further south over the last few days and\nforecast precipitation and mountain snow amounts have decreased\nsince the last forecast cycle. Nonetheless, widespread\nprecipitation will develop along and south of I-90 Sunday, with\nthe highest precipitation totals focused along the divide and in Lemhi\nCounty, ID. Valley areas will generally see less than 0.10 \ninches, with up to 0.50 inches in the mountains. \n\nAs discussed previously, snow levels will be around 7000 feet\nduring the day Sunday, but lower to 5000 feet Sunday evening. \nThis will bring minor snow accumulations to Lost Trail Pass, \nGeorgetown Lake, Homestake Pass, and passes along the Divide in \nLemhi County (i.e. Lemhi Pass), where lows by Monday morning will \nbe near freezing. Impacts will primarily be slushy accumulations \nat these locations.\n\nHigh pressure builds by Monday morning, but fog and low clouds\ncould again be impactful, especially areas that received rain the\nday before: West-central and southwest Montana, parts of central \nIdaho, and Lemhi County. \n\nChances for fog or low clouds continue Tuesday and Wednesday \nmornings, while daytime temperatures climb to slightly above-\naverage readings. The pattern becomes progressively more unsettled\nby the end of the week. Late week mountain snowfall will depend on\nhow much moisture and dynamical energy can break off from a\nlongwave trough over the northeast Pacific and move onshore, \nsimilar to what we have been experiencing lately.",
